00:12:01.901 --> 00:12:04.479
who's ever lived 
or probably ever will live. 

00:12:04.479 --> 00:12:07.197
And in some sense, 
if I can just ask a good question, 

00:12:07.227 --> 00:12:09.178
I'm the best doctor 
I've ever been in my life,

00:12:09.223 --> 00:12:11.217
and I'm not a doctor at all, right? 

00:12:11.217 --> 00:12:13.592
If I'm just stubborn, 
I can keep going and do this.

00:12:13.592 --> 00:12:20.542
And in a way, I think a lot of work over
the past 100 years was you would have

00:12:20.616 --> 00:12:24.390
some craft or subject matter expertise
that you were very, very good at.

00:12:24.731 --> 00:12:26.574
But once you hit kind 
of the boundary, right?

00:12:26.574 --> 00:12:28.664
You were a great engineer, 
but you venture into design, 

00:12:28.664 --> 00:12:31.792
you say you can have your intuition,
but ask the designer, right?

00:12:31.807 --> 00:12:35.829
Or you can have your ideas, but go
ask someone who's skilled at sales.

00:12:36.806 --> 00:12:40.719
And now, if you are stubborn enough and
pushing and willing to start to go build

00:12:40.719 --> 00:12:44.979
systems, you can extend your boundaries,
so the positive interpretation is 

00:12:44.979 --> 00:12:46.882
you're not gate-kept, 
and you can do a lot more.

00:12:46.927 --> 00:12:48.883
But I think the more 
interesting interpretation for 

00:12:48.883 --> 00:12:50.606
people building businesses is,

00:12:50.606 --> 00:12:54.907
is first, if you find really 
determined people with a high

00:12:54.907 --> 00:12:57.484
level of determination and an aptitude,

00:12:57.513 --> 00:13:00.905
just like a drive, these people can do
far more than they ever used to be able

00:13:00.905 --> 00:13:03.957
to do, so it's not restricted 
just to specific spikes.

00:13:04.372 --> 00:13:08.117
And then I think it leads you to
question, you design organizations 

00:13:08.117 --> 00:13:10.057
with lots of subspecialties, 

00:13:10.057 --> 00:13:13.316
which can lead to walls 
and barriers and things that get

00:13:13.316 --> 00:13:15.019
lost between organizations. 

00:13:15.019 --> 00:13:17.392
And I think you can radically simplify.

00:13:17.392 --> 00:13:18.700
- Wait, what does that mean?
- Yeah.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:20.252
- The barriers between organizations.

00:13:20.252 --> 00:13:24.027
-I'll put it this way. When Ramp was, call
it 10 people, and you ask people, 

00:13:24.397 --> 00:13:26.186
"Where do you work,
or what do you do here"?

00:13:26.186 --> 00:13:28.659
They'd say, "I work at Ramp, 
and I'm an engineer."

00:13:28.659 --> 00:13:31.068
You get to 50, you might hear
something simpler.

00:13:31.068 --> 00:13:33.257
"I work at Ramp, 
and I'm in the sales team."

00:13:33.257 --> 00:13:35.480
And you start seeing these
different divisions grow.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:37.799
But once you start 
getting to 200 or 500 people, 

00:13:37.799 --> 00:13:39.911
you start hearing this 
inversion in identity.

00:13:39.911 --> 00:13:42.597
People say, 
"I'm a salesperson at Ramp." 

00:13:42.597 --> 00:13:44.082
"I'm a designer at Ramp." 

00:13:44.082 --> 00:13:46.775
They start to identify by their
craft and what they do and 

00:13:46.775 --> 00:13:48.392
what they are asked to do.

00:13:48.392 --> 00:13:51.694
They interact with mostly people 
who are in their craft. 

00:13:51.694 --> 00:13:53.969
Salespeople talk with other salespeople,

00:13:53.969 --> 00:13:56.052
and you see these mega corporations.

00:13:56.052 --> 00:13:57.791
It's kind of like the Tower of Babel,

00:13:57.791 --> 00:14:01.087
where no one in sales 
knows an actual engineer. 

00:14:01.087 --> 00:14:03.017
The engineers don't know 
any actual designers.

00:14:03.032 --> 00:14:06.269
Everything is hard because 
information has to go up top and

00:14:06.269 --> 00:14:10.896
down on through, and these 
organizations become very Byzantine.

00:14:11.578 --> 00:14:16.519
And I think now in a world where someone
who develops the product can use

00:14:16.548 --> 00:14:19.669
technologies that helps them sell more
effectively, that helps them track

00:14:19.743 --> 00:14:23.008
customers, that helps them track
outreach, that helps them write with a

00:14:23.008 --> 00:14:24.571
better pitch on the first go, 

00:14:24.571 --> 00:14:27.751
and these are real tools that 
you can build for people today.

00:14:27.751 --> 00:14:31.733
I think you start seeing
fewer types of specialties within

00:14:31.733 --> 00:14:33.832
organizations, and it's leading, 

00:14:34.217 --> 00:14:36.477
I think, a lot of organizations
right now to question,

00:14:36.758 --> 00:14:40.431
"Do I have the right shape of
an organization for the world that 

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:41.772
we're starting to enter into"?

00:14:41.772 --> 00:14:44.092
And so, I just find this whole field
really interesting right now.

00:14:44.092 --> 00:14:46.908
-I want to go to the shape of organization
if you think you need to change it or not.

00:14:46.908 --> 00:14:51.046
So, what I'm understanding from 
what you're saying is like, essentially,

00:14:51.046 --> 00:14:52.339
you're looking right now in this age, 

00:14:52.339 --> 00:14:53.974
you should just find the highest agency

00:14:54.033 --> 00:14:56.755
person possible for what you're trying to
do inside your company. 

00:14:56.755 --> 00:14:57.826
I have a question for you.

00:14:57.945 --> 00:15:01.714
How do you find high agency people 
right now when you guys are hiring? 

00:15:01.714 --> 00:15:04.298
I just saw a clip from this podcast, 
which I actually really liked.

00:15:04.298 --> 00:15:05.563
It was Tobi talking about this. 

00:15:05.563 --> 00:15:07.675
And he's like, "Listen, 
I'm a high school dropout.

00:15:07.675 --> 00:15:09.027
The idea that I'm going to ask for 

00:15:09.027 --> 00:15:12.290
your credentials would be
like completely ironic."

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:16.752
And the way Shopify does it is when
they're interviewing, he's like, 

00:15:16.752 --> 00:15:18.141
"I want you to tell me your life story. 

00:15:18.141 --> 00:15:20.956
And in your life story, 
if you're a high agency

00:15:20.956 --> 00:15:24.798
person, you're going to have examples of
this thing, I ran into this blockade, and

00:15:24.798 --> 00:15:27.162
this is what I did to get over it." 

00:15:27.177 --> 00:15:29.926
And so they have high agency people reveal

00:15:29.926 --> 00:15:32.449
themselves through the telling of their
life story. How do you do it?

00:15:32.745 --> 00:15:36.086
- There are two things that
I'm generally looking for, right? 

00:15:36.101 --> 00:15:38.989
One, I completely agree with Tobi. 

00:15:38.989 --> 00:15:42.007
I think you're trying to look for
some evidence of a spike or some

00:15:42.007 --> 00:15:43.524
level of exceptional drive.

00:15:43.524 --> 00:15:46.059
I think that there are many 
people in certain fields

00:15:46.059 --> 00:15:49.314
that can be top 1% or
0.1% percent in some field. 

00:15:49.314 --> 00:15:52.044
But most are not, 
and I think that if folks are, 

00:15:52.044 --> 00:15:55.723
there's usually some evidence to 
some degree somewhere in the

00:15:55.753 --> 00:15:56.952
past that you can find.

00:15:56.952 --> 00:16:01.622
When I think about a couple of these
things, I think about there's an engineer

00:16:01.963 --> 00:16:04.807
and actually a whole litany now, a whole
community of people at Ramp, 

00:16:04.807 --> 00:16:10.238
who we found them because 
when they were 15 years old,

00:16:10.252 --> 00:16:14.223
they were playing like 80 hours, 
100 hours a week just on "Minecraft."

00:16:14.505 --> 00:16:17.752
And a few of them were known 
for building these private Minecraft 

00:16:17.752 --> 00:16:21.482
servers that were so entertaining, 
other kids would love to play it. 

00:16:21.585 --> 00:16:24.484
One of them paid his way through
college, hundreds of thousands of

00:16:24.484 --> 00:16:26.685
dollars, by making 
something that was entertaining.

00:16:26.685 --> 00:16:30.755
He became like a small business person
when he was quite literally very small.

00:16:31.392 --> 00:16:35.344
And it turns out he knew all the other
"Minecraft" developers. 

00:16:35.344 --> 00:16:36.953
And you find these people
he would have missed.

00:16:37.057 --> 00:16:41.074
I think that a lot of traditional ways of 
looking for employees would go and say,

00:16:41.074 --> 00:16:42.703
"Oh, they don't have a college degree.

00:16:42.703 --> 00:16:45.132
They aren't well-rounded
in all these ways."

00:16:45.132 --> 00:16:48.566
But he has this incredible focus and
drive, and if you talk to the other folks

00:16:48.566 --> 00:16:50.381
who are in the community 
at the time, people would say,

00:16:50.381 --> 00:16:53.486
"He was incredibly obsessive, 
he was super detailed,

00:16:53.486 --> 00:16:56.666
and he was able to bend 
the API to do things

00:16:56.666 --> 00:17:00.216
that we didn't know it could do 
because he was so discerning."

00:17:00.483 --> 00:17:04.328
And I think you can often 
find people with incredible spikes. 

00:17:04.328 --> 00:17:07.400
Sometimes it's through video games, 
sometimes it's through sports. 

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:11.873
Who has the drive to go and be that good?
It can be through grades.

00:17:12.184 --> 00:17:16.387
It can be by people who didn't have any
of these credentials, somehow are out

00:17:16.387 --> 00:17:17.655
there making and putting
out incredible work. 

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:22.220
I think even just about you, I think you 
just sat down one day a decade

00:17:22.220 --> 00:17:25.069
ago, and you said, 
"I want to make incredible podcasts." 

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:26.329
And for years and
years and years, 

00:17:26.464 --> 00:17:27.531
you were working on this, 

00:17:27.547 --> 00:17:31.188
and I just remember meeting a lot
of folks who just kept talking about,

00:17:31.247 --> 00:17:35.027
"There's this guy making Founders
podcast, and it's really good."

00:17:35.576 --> 00:17:38.853
It's all to say, I'm less interested in
what is the resume.

00:17:38.853 --> 00:17:41.534
I'm far more interested in proof of work.

00:17:41.801 --> 00:17:44.087
It can be an award. 
It can be something you built. 

00:17:44.087 --> 00:17:47.919
It can be a great work that you read and
say, "This is electric." 

00:17:47.934 --> 00:17:51.275
And so often I think 
I'm really looking for signs of that. 

00:17:51.379 --> 00:17:55.905
And you can find that I think 
by searching of where is work, 

00:17:55.905 --> 00:17:58.348
where are people producing interesting

00:17:58.348 --> 00:18:00.613
achievements, awards, bodies of work, 

00:18:00.613 --> 00:18:02.132
and actually starting 
and looking for that.

00:18:02.132 --> 00:18:07.871
And so part of our hiring process is
actually going and just trying to look

00:18:07.871 --> 00:18:12.789
for people who are very active on GitHub.
We're trying to meet people who were

00:18:12.789 --> 00:18:16.585
leaders in different bizarre
fringe communities. 

00:18:16.585 --> 00:18:20.192
And it turns out, too, even
if you have a really intensive interview

00:18:20.192 --> 00:18:23.512
process and you have 
10 rounds of interviews, right?

00:18:23.512 --> 00:18:27.274
And you interview someone for 15 hours,
it turns out in two business days of

00:18:27.274 --> 00:18:30.233
working with someone, you're going to
have more information about working with

00:18:30.233 --> 00:18:32.381
that person, so 
we rely a lot on referrals. 

00:18:32.603 --> 00:18:36.750
We try to find people who have maybe 
some asymmetric information about

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:39.416
who someone actually 
is versus who they present to be.

00:18:39.416 --> 00:18:42.576
I think the next thing which I 
specifically spend a lot of time

00:18:42.576 --> 00:18:45.720
looking for is, look,
there's a lot of incredible 

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.656
things to work on in the world, right?

00:18:47.656 --> 00:18:49.358
I shouldn't be so arrogant
to assume that people 

00:18:49.358 --> 00:18:53.112
need to want to work for Ramp or whatever
it is that we're working on, right?

00:18:53.112 --> 00:18:55.716
Or you can be working on 
making humanity multi-planetary. 

00:18:55.716 --> 00:18:59.016
You can be working 
on artificial general intelligence. 

00:18:59.016 --> 00:19:01.589
Why should I assume people should be
interested in what we're doing? 

00:19:01.589 --> 00:19:03.731
And I think a lot of that 
starts with asking,

00:19:03.731 --> 00:19:05.851
what is their actual motivation? 

00:19:05.851 --> 00:19:09.853
I think in some sense, 
like we only live our lives, right?

00:19:10.179 --> 00:19:14.401
Everyone in some sense is the hero of
their own journey, of their own story,

00:19:14.401 --> 00:19:15.794
and try to spend a lot of time.

00:19:15.794 --> 00:19:18.404
You know, forget even some 
of the capabilities, but like, 

00:19:18.404 --> 00:19:24.021
what does someone want independent of 
what Ramp will do in 5, 10, 15 years? 

00:19:24.021 --> 00:19:26.212
Where do they hope to be
in 5, 10, 15 years? 

00:19:26.212 --> 00:19:28.052
What drives them?
What motivates them?

00:19:28.052 --> 00:19:31.129
Does that coincide, and can I see and
understand how it could coincide, or

00:19:31.159 --> 00:19:36.329
present how me saying, "Actually, please
spend years of your life working on this

00:19:36.329 --> 00:19:39.214
mission, and I'll try to convince 
you why it's worthwhile."

00:19:39.259 --> 00:19:42.313
If there isn't a clear sign 
of evidence of why they might

00:19:42.313 --> 00:19:45.655
want the same thing and 
how it can connect, don't worry about it. 

00:19:45.655 --> 00:19:46.814
Don't waste your time.

00:19:46.814 --> 00:19:48.296
And so those are the two things

00:19:48.296 --> 00:19:50.505
that I spend a lot of time looking for
when I'm interviewing people.

00:19:50.771 --> 00:19:52.577
- That brings something,
a thought to my mind. 

00:19:52.725 --> 00:19:54.408
I just had a conversation with Gustav, 

00:19:54.408 --> 00:19:58.096
who's the co-CEO of Spotify, and
one thing that's remarkable about him is,

00:19:58.096 --> 00:20:02.677
he had a startup, sold a startup, then
goes to work for Spotify, then works

00:20:02.677 --> 00:20:03.811
there for 18 years, 
- Mm-hmm

00:20:03.811 --> 00:20:04.858
- then becomes the co-CEO.

00:20:05.441 --> 00:20:09.357
And if you look at the top leadership in
Spotify, it's usually, I think he said

00:20:09.357 --> 00:20:13.025
something like a decade is the average
that they've been working together.

00:20:13.025 --> 00:20:15.405
And on the other end, 
you have people like Elon,

00:20:15.405 --> 00:20:16.704
who just wants to, like.

00:20:16.704 --> 00:20:19.857
He says, "I want fresh blood. 
I want to churn through people." 

00:20:19.857 --> 00:20:21.232
What's your opinion on this?

00:20:21.232 --> 00:20:24.432
Do you want consistency in the top
people in your company, or do 

00:20:24.432 --> 00:20:25.564
you encourage turnover?

00:20:25.564 --> 00:20:28.490
Do you want to keep the core
product kernel team together?

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:31.234
Like, how do you think about that?

00:20:31.234 --> 00:20:35.720
I think that people's level of experience
can change, and you can learn unique

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:39.288
things, but I think general aptitude is
reasonably consistent.

00:20:39.288 --> 00:20:40.899
And what I mean by that is, 

00:20:40.899 --> 00:20:44.677
let's say you want to take the snob route 
and just go look at MIT 

00:20:44.677 --> 00:20:46.651
and find the smartest people there.

00:20:46.651 --> 00:20:49.297
You can reasonably figure
that out within one

00:20:49.297 --> 00:20:54.071
semester of who are the top 50 smartest
people at MIT if you ask around 

00:20:54.071 --> 00:20:55.928
and spend some time on that.

00:20:55.928 --> 00:21:00.091
And generally, people are not recruiting
people for their internship program until

00:21:00.091 --> 00:21:03.884
their junior year or their junior summer,
but you can go and say, "Actually, I'm

00:21:03.884 --> 00:21:08.261
going to try to find the smartest
freshmen and offer them a winter

00:21:08.261 --> 00:21:13.078
internship or a summer internship," and
go bring them in and find the smartest

00:21:13.152 --> 00:21:14.632
and incredible aptitude of people. 

00:21:14.647 --> 00:21:16.271
And by the way, other freshmen, 

00:21:16.271 --> 00:21:19.427
when they see the smartest person went, 
they want to go to your company, too.

00:21:19.427 --> 00:21:22.468
And you can start to create these 
virtuous cycles of finding great people.

00:21:22.468 --> 00:21:26.363
So I agree with Elon's philosophy of
trying to find really smart people, in

00:21:26.363 --> 00:21:30.701
part because it allows you to find maybe
something like a mispricing in the market.

00:21:30.701 --> 00:21:33.545
By their junior summer or after
they've been working for five years,

00:21:33.545 --> 00:21:34.331
it's priced in. 

00:21:34.331 --> 00:21:38.834
You're competing straight up
with quant firms, AI labs, whoever

00:21:38.834 --> 00:21:39.466
you name it. 

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:41.211
People are paying top dollar for folks.

00:21:41.211 --> 00:21:44.182
But that early in careers, 
when people don't have a resume, 

00:21:44.182 --> 00:21:46.028
you can start to go and break down.

00:21:46.028 --> 00:21:50.226
Actually, you can find signs of
incredible aptitude, drive, and potential

00:21:50.226 --> 00:21:52.425
for performance early on 
and start to build an affinity. 

00:21:52.425 --> 00:21:55.243
So we try to find those folks 
and give them a lot more

00:21:55.243 --> 00:21:56.831
responsibility than 
maybe they're used to. 

00:21:56.831 --> 00:22:00.282
And when they work for quite a number
of hours and do great things despite

00:22:00.282 --> 00:22:04.109
their age, and then when they've built
experience earlier in your company than

00:22:04.109 --> 00:22:05.094
they would have otherwise, 

00:22:05.109 --> 00:22:07.280
they want to stay. 
They want to build it. 

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:08.189
And that leads me to.

00:22:08.189 --> 00:22:11.193
My actual goal is I want to work
very early with people, 

00:22:11.193 --> 00:22:13.045
and I want to work with them 
for a long, long, long time.

00:22:13.445 --> 00:22:16.743
Because I think when you've built the
trust and you understand how to complete

00:22:16.743 --> 00:22:22.538
each other's sentences without the other
needing to go do it, you're able to rely

00:22:22.538 --> 00:22:26.606
on each other, you're able to move
faster, and you're able to communicate

00:22:26.606 --> 00:22:28.645
far more in a shorter period of time,

00:22:28.645 --> 00:22:31.127
which leads you to 
act with higher velocity.

00:22:31.127 --> 00:22:33.790
- It's almost like an ESP that develops.
- Yeah.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:36.023
- So, we were just talking very before we
recorded that a few days ago, 

00:22:36.023 --> 00:22:36.814
I recorded MrBeast. 

00:22:36.814 --> 00:22:39.311
The episode's not going to be
out for several months for reasons that

00:22:39.311 --> 00:22:40.330
we can't disclose. 

00:22:40.818 --> 00:22:43.965
And what's fascinating about him,
and I've spent a bunch of time

00:22:43.965 --> 00:22:49.857
with him, is he has this thing where it's
just like, he has almost like, 

00:22:51.057 --> 00:22:52.925
there's certain.

00:22:53.088 --> 00:22:55.084
The people who've worked with
him so long to the point 

00:22:55.084 --> 00:22:57.058
where they used to live with him 
- Yep.

00:22:57.058 --> 00:22:59.184
- and then spend every 
waking hour with him.

00:22:59.527 --> 00:23:01.549
And he's like, "Now..." You're not
talking about 100 hours. 

00:23:01.549 --> 00:23:05.218
In some cases, this is well 
over even 10,000 hours that

00:23:05.218 --> 00:23:06.683
they've spent working together.
- Mm-hmm.

00:23:06.683 --> 00:23:09.083
- And he says, his operations guy, 
I think his name's Tyler, 

00:23:09.083 --> 00:23:11.948
if I remember correctly,
he can walk on a set that,

00:23:12.156 --> 00:23:13.458
just like the one you're on right now,

00:23:13.665 --> 00:23:18.015
that Tyler does, and before MrBeast looks
at something or opens his mouth, 

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:18.268
Tyler's like, 

00:23:18.313 --> 00:23:20.344
"I already know what you're going
to say. I did this reason for it. 

00:23:20.344 --> 00:23:21.362
That light's over here for this. 

00:23:21.362 --> 00:23:23.330
This camera's actually here. 
You think it should be over there,

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:24.911
but the reason it's not 
over there is because

00:23:24.911 --> 00:23:25.123
- Yeah.

00:23:25.123 --> 00:23:26.873
- if I position it here,
it lets me shoot into here."

00:23:26.902 --> 00:23:29.104
And then he doesn't have to say anything.

00:23:29.104 --> 00:23:31.985
He goes, "That's exactly what 
I would have done if I knew that." 

00:23:31.985 --> 00:23:34.677
And he says, 
"It's excessively important." 

00:23:34.988 --> 00:23:36.139
And then he had a great line.

00:23:36.139 --> 00:23:37.495
He's like, "You don't
get there in 100 hours."

00:23:37.495 --> 00:23:37.775
- Yeah.

00:23:37.775 --> 00:23:40.869
- There is something special about this,
like I talked to Charlie Munger. 

00:23:40.869 --> 00:23:43.532
I went to Charlie Munger's house right
before he died, and the fascinating thing,

00:23:43.532 --> 00:23:45.949
one of the questions that one of
these other entrepreneurs were 

00:23:45.949 --> 00:23:46.812
with asked him is like,

00:23:46.812 --> 00:23:48.237
"Well, how often do you talk
to Buffett these days?" 

00:23:48.237 --> 00:23:48.919
And he's like, 

00:23:48.919 --> 00:23:50.333
"We talked so much, we got to the point

00:23:50.422 --> 00:23:52.856
where we didn't have to 
call each other anymore."

00:23:52.856 --> 00:23:53.508
- Yeah.

00:23:53.508 --> 00:23:54.447
- And his whole point was just like, 

00:23:54.447 --> 00:23:56.286
"I already know. 
Buffett knows.

00:23:56.286 --> 00:23:59.566
He used to organize 
his thoughts with me. 

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.510
Now he knows what I'm going 
to say, so he could be like, 

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:03.278
'Oh, I should pick up the phone
and call Charlie,' and then have the

00:24:03.278 --> 00:24:06.508
conversation in his head without actually
having the conversation."

00:24:06.508 --> 00:24:10.010
It allows, I think, a much more pure
pursuit of the mission, because 

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:12.940
you understand what 
each other are pursuing,

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:14.866
what that mission actually means, 

00:24:14.866 --> 00:24:17.129
what the impact is if you're successful,

00:24:17.159 --> 00:24:19.211
and you also know 
what you are excellent at

00:24:19.522 --> 00:24:21.895
and you can take and push forward, 

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:25.080
and what you can rely on 
another person to do with you.

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:27.845
And I think it leads to these
effects where you see some companies,

00:24:27.845 --> 00:24:29.765
when you have a really strong culture,
you have really strong trust,

00:24:29.765 --> 00:24:32.628
it's not necessarily that the
talent level is different.

00:24:32.628 --> 00:24:34.343
It's that in every single hour, 

00:24:34.343 --> 00:24:37.989
the amount of throughput that 
organization gets is radically higher. 

00:24:37.989 --> 00:24:42.186
And so, I think some form of 
both is what we're trying to pursue. 

00:24:42.186 --> 00:24:44.763
And then last, look, I think one
of the most damaging things 

00:24:44.763 --> 00:24:46.459
you can do to an organization is

00:24:46.459 --> 00:24:50.128
if there are folks who
are getting paid similarly,

00:24:50.143 --> 00:24:52.507
have similar ownership, 
and are just not pulling their weight.

00:24:52.537 --> 00:24:58.547
Because I think it leads to this very
tough question and fair one for folks of

00:24:58.547 --> 00:25:04.207
like, if I'm busting my a** and working
super hard and giving up on all the

00:25:04.281 --> 00:25:10.181
things that I am, and there's all these
folks free-riding and you're not

00:25:10.285 --> 00:25:11.993
enforcing high standards, 

00:25:13.342 --> 00:25:18.568
why should we obsess so much 
over reaching our higher potential?

00:25:18.568 --> 00:25:23.156
This organization doesn't seem to
demonstrate a real respect for the

00:25:23.823 --> 00:25:28.208
pursuit of higher and higher standards
and an intolerance for anything less than

00:25:28.208 --> 00:25:29.007
giving your best. 

00:25:29.096 --> 00:25:31.699
Like, ultimately, I want to win,
but what motivates and

00:25:31.699 --> 00:25:36.803
drives me and most people at Ramp is this
belief that every day we can wake up and

00:25:36.803 --> 00:25:39.897
actually get a little bit better, drive a
little bit more impact, get better 

00:25:39.897 --> 00:25:40.927
at the things that we do.

00:25:40.927 --> 00:25:43.333
And I think when you have an
organization filled with people 

00:25:43.718 --> 00:25:47.365
who share those set of beliefs, 
iron sharpens iron. 

00:25:47.988 --> 00:25:49.836
You build better products faster. 

00:25:49.836 --> 00:25:52.303
And so my whole job,

00:25:52.303 --> 00:25:54.984
I don't believe I'm the 
smartest person at Ramp. 

00:25:54.984 --> 00:25:58.214
Not even close, sadly, at this point.

00:25:58.925 --> 00:26:02.155
My job is to try to create the conditions
for people to do their life's work, 

00:26:02.392 --> 00:26:05.639
and that involves finding great people,
making Ramp the kind of place that people

00:26:05.639 --> 00:26:07.317
want to come and do their great work. 

00:26:07.347 --> 00:26:09.166
And then once they're here, ensuring that

00:26:09.166 --> 00:26:13.379
they're able to do incredible work, make
decisions quickly, see if they're right

00:26:13.379 --> 00:26:16.774
or wrong fast, and get products into
people's hands and move. 

00:26:16.774 --> 00:26:19.027
That's my job, is to
unblock these other people.

