Ramp is six years old, seven.
How many years right now?

00:27:34.530 --> 00:27:34.710
- Mm-hmm.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.226
- I know you do the day thing,
but how many years?

00:27:36.226 --> 00:27:37.906
It's just over 7 years 
- Okay.

00:27:37.906 --> 00:27:40.521
or 2,620 days, 
in case you were curious.

00:27:40.521 --> 00:27:41.372
There you go.

00:27:41.372 --> 00:27:43.828
So, seven years ago, and
you guys had this from day one, 

00:27:43.828 --> 00:27:49.786
where you're doing expense management,
corporate cards, B2B, but you guys had a

00:27:49.786 --> 00:27:55.827
love and insistence on elegance of
design, almost like a consumer level that

00:27:56.020 --> 00:27:57.029
you didn't see in 
- Yeah.

00:27:57.118 --> 00:27:59.451
- B2B software, which is still talked 
about to this day and has

00:27:59.451 --> 00:28:01.592
been copied by many
other companies.

00:28:01.592 --> 00:28:01.946
- Sure.

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:04.129
- But, yeah, I think that
just speaks to you.

00:28:04.129 --> 00:28:06.563
It's like, if we want to
have excellent standards, 

00:28:06.563 --> 00:28:08.949
I'm going to quote
Tobi for the third f***ing time

00:28:08.949 --> 00:28:10.619
in this podcast, but he even goes, 

00:28:10.619 --> 00:28:12.384
"I hate these goddamn Norman doors." 

00:28:12.384 --> 00:28:13.355
He has thick accent.
- Yes. 

00:28:13.355 --> 00:28:14.741
- I was like, "What is that?"
- Yeah.

00:28:14.741 --> 00:28:15.714
I didn't know what that was, 

00:28:15.714 --> 00:28:17.640
and it's a door where you can't tell
if you should push or pull.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:18.060
- Exactly.

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:21.851
- And so he's like, "I can't have shitty
design things," so he had them removed

00:28:21.851 --> 00:28:23.570
from the Shopify 
offices because he's like,

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:27.097
"That design falls below
the engineering standards that 

00:28:27.097 --> 00:28:30.840
I'm trying to get to, like 
I want spread, and we have to adhere to."

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:33.457
And I love this idea of like, 
the way you do one thing is the way

00:28:33.457 --> 00:28:34.457
you do everything.

00:28:34.457 --> 00:28:36.338
- It's totally right, and it's funny,

00:28:36.338 --> 00:28:39.239
like a group of designers were
hanging out the other day,

00:28:39.239 --> 00:28:41.513
and I found myself in a
very funny conversation.

00:28:41.513 --> 00:28:44.243
Have you ever used a Breville toaster?
- No.

00:28:44.243 --> 00:28:46.323
- If you care about design,
you're meeting all these people,

00:28:46.323 --> 00:28:47.962
you should buy a Breville toaster.

00:28:48.252 --> 00:28:51.672
So one of the big problems,
right, in enterprise software,

00:28:51.672 --> 00:28:55.660
in a lot of B2B software,
is it starts out clean and elegant.

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:58.282
There are a bunch of businesses
who love the product

00:28:58.282 --> 00:28:59.918
and they start serving more customers,

00:28:59.928 --> 00:29:01.995
and they have more requirements,
and they have more ideas.

00:29:01.995 --> 00:29:05.560
And before you know it, you end up
with these disasters of B2B softwares,

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:10.493
which require a PhD to
learn how to use these tools,

00:29:10.493 --> 00:29:12.963
and people who will
maintain your database.

00:29:12.963 --> 00:29:15.914
They're horrible to use, and as a result,
you never want to go log in.

00:29:15.914 --> 00:29:18.633
You can kind of name
what other software is.

00:29:18.633 --> 00:29:21.128
And for salespeople, for HR people,

00:29:21.128 --> 00:29:23.233
for finance people,
you name it, they're horrible.

00:29:23.233 --> 00:29:26.688
That's, I think, a lot of how people
would describe B2B software.

00:29:26.958 --> 00:29:28.603
Let's go back to toasting, right?

00:29:28.603 --> 00:29:29.717
If you were to go ask people,

00:29:29.717 --> 00:29:31.598
"What do you want in a toaster
or maybe in a microwave?"

00:29:31.598 --> 00:29:37.153
They might say, "I like toast,
but sometimes I want to reheat pizza.

00:29:37.413 --> 00:29:39.566
I should have a reheat frozen pizza mode."

00:29:40.096 --> 00:29:45.873
Or maybe you're cooking stuff and
you want it to be more like an oven,

00:29:45.873 --> 00:29:47.167
or you want to make popcorn,

00:29:47.167 --> 00:29:49.993
and it's a toaster/microwave,
and all these kinds of things.

00:29:49.993 --> 00:29:53.552
Before you know it, these toasters are
filled with buttons and buttons

00:29:53.552 --> 00:29:55.220
and buttons and knobs and dials,
- Yeah.

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:57.270
- and it's super crazy, right?

00:29:57.270 --> 00:30:00.338
And I think that is quite literally
asking customers what they want

00:30:00.338 --> 00:30:01.613
and building exactly that.

00:30:01.793 --> 00:30:04.039
The Breville toaster I find
is very, very interesting.

00:30:04.699 --> 00:30:06.962
I think there was an
incredible designer there

00:30:06.962 --> 00:30:10.833
who thought about things maybe
like Tobi did with the Norman doors.

00:30:11.373 --> 00:30:14.682
And what he started doing is try to
observe people toasting things.

00:30:15.092 --> 00:30:17.226
And if you watch people toast things,

00:30:17.776 --> 00:30:20.882
what they actually are doing
basically falls into two categories.

00:30:20.882 --> 00:30:26.279
You hit start, and either you've
undercooked the toast,

00:30:26.279 --> 00:30:28.463
and you need to put it
back in for another cycle,

00:30:28.743 --> 00:30:31.780
or you've overdone it, and it's black,
you need to start over, and it's horrible.

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:34.272
And so if you look at that,
there really is only a few buttons,

00:30:34.272 --> 00:30:37.921
and one of the four buttons, I think,
on there is a button called A Bit More.

00:30:38.701 --> 00:30:42.717
The reason it goes back to that is
actually one of the most dominant things,

00:30:42.717 --> 00:30:45.132
and no one would ask you,
right, for a bit more.

00:30:45.132 --> 00:30:46.980
They just want the perfect level of toast,

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:49.353
and it's hard to kind of know that,
and so they built that in.

00:30:49.353 --> 00:30:52.368
And I think that great design is
following and understanding.

00:30:53.368 --> 00:30:56.340
No one is coming to Ramp saying,
"I want to buy a credit card,"

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:58.263
or "Can you build me a
better expense app?"

00:30:58.263 --> 00:31:00.980
I don't think people should do
expense reports. It's crazy.

00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:03.419
All the data is digital
in the first place.

00:31:04.259 --> 00:31:08.978
You're an adult who's allowed to buy
things on behalf of your company.

00:31:08.978 --> 00:31:10.648
Expenses should do themselves, right?

00:31:10.648 --> 00:31:12.385
If we have a world that
computers can think,

00:31:12.385 --> 00:31:14.083
your books should close themselves, right?

00:31:14.083 --> 00:31:18.337
And so a lot of what we were trying to
do was not be stuck in the form factor

00:31:18.337 --> 00:31:19.899
of what is the product
we're trying to solve,

00:31:19.899 --> 00:31:22.615
but what are we trying to
deliver for customers?

00:31:22.615 --> 00:31:27.482
We are trying to deliver a business that
is easier to run, that is more profitable,

00:31:27.482 --> 00:31:32.611
that is not distracting you
with bullshit at work.

00:31:32.611 --> 00:31:35.670
And so I'm happy it's manifested
in terms of people saying,

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:37.343
"Ramp is easy to use, intuitive,"

00:31:37.343 --> 00:31:40.262
but I think if you actually
respect people's time

00:31:40.262 --> 00:31:41.697
and care about what they're doing,

00:31:42.487 --> 00:31:44.839
you have to be asking
these kinds of questions.

00:31:44.839 --> 00:31:46.602
You have to be interested
in these kinds of products.

00:31:46.963 --> 00:31:48.375
- This leads me to another question.

00:31:48.825 --> 00:31:51.948
How do you align incentives
with your customers?

00:31:51.948 --> 00:31:55.909
One of my favorite lines from the history
of entrepreneurship comes from Henry Ford.

00:31:55.909 --> 00:31:58.476
He says, "Money comes
naturally as a result of service."

00:31:58.476 --> 00:31:59.596
So in his organization, he's like,

00:31:59.596 --> 00:32:00.918
"We're going to focus on
serving the customers,

00:32:00.918 --> 00:32:02.641
and the money will take care of
itself as long as you do that."

00:32:02.641 --> 00:32:05.281
Me and you were at an event,
we were talking at an event together,

00:32:05.281 --> 00:32:06.710
I don't know, a month or two ago.

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:07.851
You were sitting to my right.

00:32:08.601 --> 00:32:11.064
You were wearing Ramp merch,

00:32:11.064 --> 00:32:15.590
and I grab your sleeve because
I wanted to read what was on it.

00:32:15.590 --> 00:32:17.393
It says, "We win when our customers win."

00:32:17.393 --> 00:32:19.677
- Yeah.
- So, I believe that to be true.

00:32:19.677 --> 00:32:22.690
So how do you align the incentives of
your company with your customers

00:32:22.690 --> 00:32:24.103
to make sure that stays true?

00:32:24.553 --> 00:32:26.480
It's one of our six values,

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:29.192
and a lot of companies have this
idea that they want to have values.

00:32:29.652 --> 00:32:31.510
My elementary school had values, right?

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:35.961
What I didn't like about the
values at my elementary school,

00:32:36.401 --> 00:32:38.389
sorry if any administrators
are listening to it.

00:32:38.389 --> 00:32:41.368
There were like 20 of them, and I
couldn't remember any of them, right?

00:32:41.368 --> 00:32:44.528
And what we tried to do at the
very start of the company was ask,

00:32:44.528 --> 00:32:48.758
"What are the very few things
that all of us really agree with?"

00:32:48.758 --> 00:32:52.412
And if we don't all strongly feel this
and agree with this, it's a nice idea.

00:32:52.871 --> 00:32:55.720
You can go pursue it, but it's
not an actual value, right?

00:32:55.720 --> 00:32:59.262
And so one of the top-level things we
wanted to do at the start of the company

00:32:59.262 --> 00:33:04.078
was set a goal that explicitly measured,
for the products that we put out there,

00:33:04.078 --> 00:33:07.036
could we measure how many dollars
are our customers spending,

00:33:07.886 --> 00:33:09.901
and how many did we block?

00:33:10.311 --> 00:33:15.554
How many dollars did we prevent
from going out of their company?

00:33:15.924 --> 00:33:19.595
How many hours did they spend
doing expenses or closing their books

00:33:19.595 --> 00:33:22.962
or paying bills or moving funds
to earn higher yield?

00:33:23.572 --> 00:33:26.626
And so we both...
It's part of why we connect, right,

00:33:26.626 --> 00:33:30.697
to a company's accounting software
to have kind of a source of truth

00:33:31.067 --> 00:33:32.225
as part of the way we move funds,

00:33:32.225 --> 00:33:38.659
and then we explicitly create measurements
on how many dollars did we block,

00:33:39.229 --> 00:33:41.183
how many expense reports
did we automate, all that.

00:33:41.593 --> 00:33:46.672
And then all we do each and every month
is look at the scoreboard, and we ask,

00:33:47.163 --> 00:33:50.465
"Did we save in aggregate
customers more money?

00:33:51.145 --> 00:33:52.873
Did we save them more time?

00:33:52.873 --> 00:33:56.428
And for individual customers, did the
experience get better or worse?"

00:33:56.428 --> 00:33:59.549
And our job is to make sure
the products that we build

00:33:59.549 --> 00:34:02.132
ultimately ladder back to that goal.

00:34:02.492 --> 00:34:06.265
For example, automating merchant matching
or being able to go and take, like...

00:34:06.265 --> 00:34:08.522
Often, if you look at your credit card
statement today, for most people,

00:34:08.522 --> 00:34:10.483
it's like a string of text,
and it's inscrutable.

00:34:10.483 --> 00:34:13.451
It'll say like "UBR STAR 478,"

00:34:13.451 --> 00:34:16.305
and you have finance people
whose time is wasted

00:34:16.305 --> 00:34:17.697
running pivot tables to tell you, 

00:34:17.697 --> 00:34:21.952
"Oh, that's Uber, and we spent
50,000 dollars this month at Uber."

00:34:21.952 --> 00:34:26.398
With Ramp, part of why we built that
was we discovered our end customers,

00:34:26.398 --> 00:34:27.831
their finance teams were just going

00:34:27.831 --> 00:34:30.107
and trying to do all these
transformations to simply say,

00:34:30.107 --> 00:34:32.439
"Oh, we spent money at Uber.
Here's what we spent."

00:34:32.439 --> 00:34:34.136
And so part of what we did was,

00:34:34.536 --> 00:34:36.823
after measuring where people were
going and spending their time,

00:34:36.823 --> 00:34:39.143
we built software to
automate that for people.

00:34:39.143 --> 00:34:41.104
Okay, we've saved an hour here for them.

00:34:41.104 --> 00:34:44.587
We've saved this hour for
20,000 other companies.

00:34:44.587 --> 00:34:47.703
How do we start adding more
features and compounding?"

00:34:47.703 --> 00:34:50.416
And so you flash forward seven years.

00:34:50.416 --> 00:34:53.792
Today, any customer you talk to
who uses Ramp will tell you,

00:34:53.792 --> 00:34:56.578
"This thing saved me way more
hours than I expected it to.

00:34:56.578 --> 00:34:59.005
It saved me time in areas I didn't expect.

00:34:59.005 --> 00:35:00.375
I was able to block transactions,

00:35:00.375 --> 00:35:03.677
so when I canceled my subscription
to some SaaS software

00:35:03.677 --> 00:35:06.776
and I deactivated that merchant just
on the card, it didn't go through."

00:35:06.776 --> 00:35:09.986
We are tracking those
religiously across the company

00:35:09.986 --> 00:35:12.073
to try to go and get better
at it each and every month.

00:35:12.073 --> 00:35:14.268
And as good as Ramp is,
I don't think it's good enough.

00:35:14.268 --> 00:35:18.075
I think we can do a lot more just to
cut out waste and wasted motion.

00:35:18.075 --> 00:35:20.711
- A question on the scoreboard.
They use the term scoreboard.

00:35:20.711 --> 00:35:22.697
How is this presented to your team?
- Yeah.

00:35:22.697 --> 00:35:23.719
- Is it an actual scoreboard

00:35:23.719 --> 00:35:24.735
inside of Ramp?
- Yes.

00:35:24.735 --> 00:35:29.059
Yes. Yeah, I mean, you can get it
any minute or second of the day

00:35:29.059 --> 00:35:31.082
if you go onto our internal dashboards,

00:35:31.082 --> 00:35:34.689
or you can query,
ask one of our internal agents,

00:35:34.689 --> 00:35:36.529
Ramp Research, any question you want.

00:35:37.038 --> 00:35:40.811
"How many dollars and hours
did we save a specific customer?

00:35:40.811 --> 00:35:41.811
Show me all the ways."

00:35:41.811 --> 00:35:44.215
And it's tallying, but
effectively it's a data table.

00:35:44.215 --> 00:35:46.235
- But it's inside a dashboard
on their computer?

00:35:46.235 --> 00:35:47.292
- Yes.
- Have you ever thought about

00:35:48.082 --> 00:35:49.800
making a giant version on the wall?
- Yeah.

00:35:50.328 --> 00:35:51.775
It's on the wall.
- Okay.

00:35:51.775 --> 00:35:55.612
- It's reported out in the
largest channels in Slack.

00:35:55.612 --> 00:35:58.355
It's things that we talk
about frankly every month.

00:35:58.355 --> 00:36:02.264
And it's part of why,
even on the first slide,

00:36:02.494 --> 00:36:05.220
if we're meeting with
a new potential prospect,

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:07.595
we talk not just about how many dollars,

00:36:07.595 --> 00:36:09.815
how many customers are using it,
how many dollars we've moved,

00:36:09.815 --> 00:36:13.850
but how many fewer dollars and hours
have we helped organizations spend?

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:15.338
- Yeah, I think putting
that data in front,

00:36:15.338 --> 00:36:17.165
constantly reminding and putting
it in front of your employees,

00:36:17.165 --> 00:36:18.612
is super important.

00:36:19.182 --> 00:36:20.975
I did this episode on
Ken Griffin from Citadel.

00:36:20.975 --> 00:36:23.427
- Yeah.
- And I think he was giving this lecture

00:36:23.427 --> 00:36:25.980
at Yale, or maybe it was Stanford,
and I thought it was interesting.

00:36:26.440 --> 00:36:28.884
He actually got the idea
from Saudi Aramco.

00:36:28.884 --> 00:36:29.913
- Yeah.
- And he's like-

00:36:29.913 --> 00:36:30.971
- Huh.
- "There's not..."

00:36:30.971 --> 00:36:33.825
Like you said, if you have
20 variables, it's too many.

00:36:33.825 --> 00:36:35.468
- Yeah.
- Six, five, whatever it is.

00:36:35.468 --> 00:36:39.100
So Saudi would put up the handful
of things that were most important,

00:36:39.100 --> 00:36:41.041
like maybe how much
oil they drilled that day

00:36:41.041 --> 00:36:42.953
or how many they shipped,
or whatever the case was,

00:36:43.273 --> 00:36:46.309
and it was a giant f***ing
screen in their headquarters.

00:36:46.309 --> 00:36:47.314
- Yep.
- And at the time,

00:36:47.314 --> 00:36:48.822
I think Ken was having an issue with risk,

00:36:48.822 --> 00:36:50.808
and he identified a handful of
things that we need to monitor,

00:36:50.808 --> 00:36:55.374
and he said he drastically
reduced their risk by just putting it...

00:36:55.374 --> 00:36:56.960
I think at the time
Citadel was in Chicago,

00:36:56.960 --> 00:36:59.714
just putting this giant dashboard,
like a scoreboard-

00:36:59.714 --> 00:37:00.716
- Mm-hmm.
- in the office where

00:37:00.716 --> 00:37:01.725
everybody could see it.
- Yeah.

00:37:01.725 --> 00:37:03.565
- Just this constant reminder.
It's like you can't run from it.

00:37:03.565 --> 00:37:07.039
- That's it. And it comes back to,
at the end of the day,

00:37:07.779 --> 00:37:12.632
people love complexity in
new things and new products,

00:37:12.632 --> 00:37:13.701
and the world is changing.

00:37:13.701 --> 00:37:15.285
There's nothing wrong with that.

00:37:15.756 --> 00:37:18.230
In some sense,
a company is just a fiction.

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:20.420
You make some filings
somewhere and you say,

00:37:20.420 --> 00:37:21.949
"We're a corporation today,"

00:37:21.949 --> 00:37:24.446
even though we're just two
people sitting around a couch

00:37:24.446 --> 00:37:25.455
and we have a few dollars-
- Mm-hmm.

00:37:25.455 --> 00:37:27.055
- in a bank account, right?

00:37:27.055 --> 00:37:31.413
And if these fictions are intended to
grow to hundreds, to thousands,

00:37:31.413 --> 00:37:33.159
to tens of thousands of people,

00:37:33.659 --> 00:37:36.823
it should be because there is
some common purpose.

00:37:36.823 --> 00:37:38.978
There is something that, in aggregate,

00:37:38.978 --> 00:37:41.372
the sum of the parts of all
the people working together

00:37:41.372 --> 00:37:45.623
should be able to move something further,
faster than anyone alone could do.

00:37:45.623 --> 00:37:49.180
And so I think part of the role of
a leader, frankly, is to go and assert,

00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:51.523
"How will we measure ourselves?"

00:37:51.523 --> 00:37:54.513
If we are seeking to be our
best and get better every day,

00:37:55.283 --> 00:37:56.580
how should we think about that,

00:37:56.580 --> 00:37:59.555
and how can we understand
our forward progress?

00:37:59.555 --> 00:38:00.761
What is the right scoreboard?

00:38:00.761 --> 00:38:03.662
And so, yeah, we spend an
incredible amount of time on that,

00:38:03.662 --> 00:38:04.698
and at the end of the day,

00:38:04.968 --> 00:38:07.706
I don't know what Ramp will
look like exactly in 100 years,

00:38:07.706 --> 00:38:11.142
but I think, just as it
was true 100 years ago,

00:38:11.142 --> 00:38:14.154
people always wanted more
out of every dollar and hour.

00:38:14.154 --> 00:38:18.932
I think people will absolutely want it in
100 years, in whatever form that may take.

00:38:18.932 --> 00:38:21.326
And so I think for...
- You're speaking to this Bezos idea

00:38:21.346 --> 00:38:22.517
where it's like-
- Yeah.

00:38:22.937 --> 00:38:24.623
- one of the smartest things
a company can do

00:38:24.623 --> 00:38:26.954
is identify the things that don't change,

00:38:26.954 --> 00:38:29.433
and he identified three
at the beginning of Amazon.

00:38:29.433 --> 00:38:32.856
And if you can invest heavily
in them decade after decade-

00:38:32.856 --> 00:38:34.299
- That's it.
- because you know

00:38:34.299 --> 00:38:37.945
the customer's going to want lower prices,
more selection, and faster delivery.

00:38:37.945 --> 00:38:39.443
- That's it. That's it.

00:38:39.443 --> 00:38:42.169
And look, there's so much
that's changing about the world,

00:38:42.169 --> 00:38:45.736
and we can talk about what we're
focused on and obsessed in

00:38:45.736 --> 00:38:48.595
and some of the themes of this decade,

00:38:48.595 --> 00:38:54.335
but I totally subscribe to that notion of
you should be focusing on the timeless

00:38:54.335 --> 00:38:56.311
if you're trying to build
an enduring brand.

00:38:56.311 --> 00:38:58.815
I want this to be the last
company I ever work on,

00:38:58.815 --> 00:39:00.522
and I want to work on this
for the rest of my life.

00:39:01.093 --> 00:39:02.745
I love building this thing.

00:39:02.745 --> 00:39:03.767
You know I was going to
ask you that question.

00:39:03.767 --> 00:39:06.249
Like, I talk to founders and
they want to talk to me...

00:39:06.249 --> 00:39:07.505
So we talk about their company and-
- Yeah.

00:39:07.505 --> 00:39:09.051
- one of the first things
I always ask is like,

00:39:09.051 --> 00:39:11.254
"Is this your last company?"
- Yeah.

00:39:11.254 --> 00:39:13.307
- It's just that's the thing
I'm super interested in,

00:39:13.307 --> 00:39:15.767
because then all the good things
in life come in compounding.

00:39:15.767 --> 00:39:19.125
And I was just at this
dinner two nights ago

00:39:19.125 --> 00:39:25.516
and ran into the son of this guy I wound
up being able to spend an hour with,

00:39:25.516 --> 00:39:28.790
and just sold his company
for 50 billion dollars.

00:39:28.790 --> 00:39:30.675
I think he's 77 or something like that.
- Mm-hmm.

00:39:30.675 --> 00:39:32.325
- And he started that
company when he was 22.

00:39:32.325 --> 00:39:35.109
So that means he's 
had 55 years of experience 

00:39:35.109 --> 00:39:37.265
in the same company.
- Yeah.

00:39:37.265 --> 00:39:39.347
- And it's like those
conversations are so full.

00:39:39.347 --> 00:39:40.409
Like, insight per minute,

00:39:40.409 --> 00:39:42.229
you're not going to beat that at all.
- Mm-hmm.

00:39:42.669 --> 00:39:47.703
That's it. And you'll see things at a
deeper level of depth, and it's fun.

00:39:48.593 --> 00:39:53.044
You learn. All the scar tissue
you have, the mistakes you made,

00:39:53.044 --> 00:39:55.935
I think, allow you to move with
a lot more efficiency and movement.

00:39:56.104 --> 00:40:00.555
- I am very skeptical of the statement
that humans repeat throughout history

00:40:00.555 --> 00:40:02.244
that this time is different.
- Hmm.

00:40:02.244 --> 00:40:03.724
- Usually it's not different.

00:40:03.724 --> 00:40:07.701
I was spending more time with
Michael Dell the other day, and I figured,

00:40:07.701 --> 00:40:08.809
"Okay, you started the
company when you were 19.

00:40:08.809 --> 00:40:09.825
You're in your 60s."

00:40:09.825 --> 00:40:11.351
He's had to manage and thrive

00:40:11.351 --> 00:40:13.966
through all these different technological
revolutions and everything else.

00:40:13.966 --> 00:40:17.602
I go, "Michael, is this time different?"
He goes, "Yes. It's actually different."

00:40:17.602 --> 00:40:19.216
We had a wonderful conversation about it.

00:40:19.216 --> 00:40:23.784
So I want to go back to AI has to...
- Yep.

00:40:23.784 --> 00:40:25.590
- I would imagine, in your opinion,

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:27.540
and I've spent enough time with you,
I think I know the answer to this,

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:31.445
but I think it's going to change the
shape of your organization, is it not?

00:40:32.205 --> 00:40:33.474
- I think this is a super deep topic.

00:40:33.474 --> 00:40:37.733
And maybe we'll just start at the
highest level, like I think you'd ask,

00:40:37.733 --> 00:40:42.141
"Does AI throw out all the fundamentals
and lessons we've learned about business?"

00:40:42.141 --> 00:40:46.541
And I say this because I'll encounter
engineers every month, right?

00:40:46.541 --> 00:40:50.950
On one side, you have
people who are terrified, right?

00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:54.373
This is doom, and it's going to be kind of
the end of business as we know it.

00:40:54.373 --> 00:40:57.856
At the other, you see
almost this obsession

00:40:57.856 --> 00:41:00.462
with the labs and deep love of them.

00:41:00.462 --> 00:41:04.500
And look, I think there's something
incredibly revolutionary going on.

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:05.679
I think that some of the labs

00:41:05.679 --> 00:41:10.222
are going to be some of the
best businesses created of all time.

00:41:10.222 --> 00:41:13.727
But the potential of AI is so great
it can blind people and ask,

00:41:13.727 --> 00:41:15.684
"Is it worth working on anything else?

00:41:15.684 --> 00:41:18.893
Should we even build a company if
AGI is almost here around the corner?"

00:41:18.893 --> 00:41:22.276
And I think history offers some
pretty interesting lessons around this.

00:41:22.276 --> 00:41:24.666
And maybe, to use an example,
let's talk about air conditioning.

00:41:24.666 --> 00:41:27.099
It's 95 degrees outside.

00:41:27.099 --> 00:41:28.752
We're not sitting here talking about

00:41:28.752 --> 00:41:33.148
the great robber baron families
of the air conditioning industry, right?

00:41:33.148 --> 00:41:35.762
And these great family fortunes
of the people who created it.

00:41:35.762 --> 00:41:37.795
And at the same time,
like air conditioning...

00:41:37.795 --> 00:41:38.886
I was born in Las Vegas. 

00:41:38.886 --> 00:41:40.989
That city wouldn't exist if not
for the air conditioner. 

00:41:40.989 --> 00:41:42.425
You live in Miami, right?

00:41:42.425 --> 00:41:43.924
There's entire ecosystems.

00:41:43.924 --> 00:41:45.561
If you think about it,
the value that was created

00:41:45.561 --> 00:41:48.062
out of the ecosystems far exceeded

00:41:48.062 --> 00:41:51.925
the value that was captured by the
people who invented this technology.

00:41:51.925 --> 00:41:54.423
And I think with artificial intelligence,

00:41:54.423 --> 00:41:56.280
don't get me wrong,
I think that this is going to be

00:41:56.280 --> 00:41:59.683
one of the most revolutionary
transformations in our life.

00:41:59.683 --> 00:42:02.465
I think on the scale beyond
the Industrial Revolution,

00:42:02.465 --> 00:42:05.196
making goods plentiful,
and it'll make services plentiful.

00:42:05.196 --> 00:42:09.847
I think it is interesting to go work on
creating a general intelligence

00:42:09.847 --> 00:42:11.665
that is artificial for the world.

00:42:11.665 --> 00:42:14.985
But I think one of the more
interesting questions to be asking is,

00:42:14.985 --> 00:42:18.488
in a world in which
intelligence is very plentiful,

00:42:18.488 --> 00:42:22.042
in which intelligence is accessible,
in which intelligence is cheap, 

00:42:22.042 --> 00:42:23.047
what should you build?

00:42:23.047 --> 00:42:25.381
What kind of businesses are now possible?

00:42:25.741 --> 00:42:29.054
Just as there were cities and
things created that were not possible

00:42:29.054 --> 00:42:31.488
before maybe the air conditioner
and something as dull as that,

00:42:31.488 --> 00:42:34.415
I think that there are entire
things that we can go do

00:42:34.415 --> 00:42:37.050
when you have intelligence
that is accessible.

00:42:37.810 --> 00:42:38.852
Come back to us.

00:42:38.852 --> 00:42:42.932
In some sense, you and I are a
form of general intelligence, right?

00:42:42.932 --> 00:42:45.413
We have kind of our evolved monkey brains,

00:42:45.893 --> 00:42:48.540
and we've kind of seen over the
course of thousands of years,

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:50.891
how does a general
intelligence like to do things?

00:42:50.891 --> 00:42:54.794
And it turns out that general
intelligences like to pay for things.

00:42:54.794 --> 00:42:57.628
They will hire other types of
intelligences to go do this.

00:42:57.628 --> 00:43:00.028
And you can think about payments almost as

00:43:00.028 --> 00:43:05.734
a form of a distributed ledger to
manage resources and work.

00:43:05.734 --> 00:43:10.415
There's records around this, and there's a
reallocation of these types of resources.

00:43:10.415 --> 00:43:13.644
And I think part of why we
are so interested in payments

00:43:13.644 --> 00:43:17.300
in this kind of world is I think when
you have an explosion of intelligence

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:18.649
and it's more accessible than ever,

00:43:18.649 --> 00:43:20.852
I think you will have an
explosion of payments,

00:43:20.852 --> 00:43:23.004
whether it's people or
agents on behalf of people,

00:43:23.004 --> 00:43:25.378
or even agents in
general making purchases.

00:43:25.378 --> 00:43:28.774
And I think you'll need a better
substrate and a form factor

00:43:28.774 --> 00:43:32.864
to track, control, and get more
value out of every dollar and hour.

00:43:32.864 --> 00:43:35.224
And so a lot of what we're
focused on right now is,

00:43:35.224 --> 00:43:40.393
"How do you build the scaffolding
and the systems for organizations

00:43:40.393 --> 00:43:44.143
to manage not just the dollars they're
spending on people and things,

00:43:44.143 --> 00:43:48.955
but the dollars they're ultimately
spending toward resources, toward tokens?"

00:43:48.955 --> 00:43:50.756
- Talk about what you guys
are doing with token spend.

00:43:50.756 --> 00:43:51.817
This is very interesting.

00:43:52.127 --> 00:43:53.900
- I want to be clear,
this is not just some

00:43:53.900 --> 00:43:55.855
esoteric science fiction topic, right?

00:43:56.115 --> 00:43:57.132
Just observe it.

00:43:57.132 --> 00:43:58.481
Like, I don't know,
let's take Anthropic.

00:43:58.481 --> 00:44:00.595
Three years ago,
they made their first dollar.

00:44:00.995 --> 00:44:02.346
While we're recording this,

00:44:02.346 --> 00:44:07.036
the rumor is the run-rate revenue
is over 50 billion dollars a year.

00:44:07.036 --> 00:44:10.298
And if you play out what they
and OpenAI, just the two labs,

00:44:10.298 --> 00:44:13.301
are on track to do in revenue,
and call it a year's time,

00:44:13.861 --> 00:44:16.173
I think it'd be reasonably
conservative at this point

00:44:16.173 --> 00:44:19.278
to say they're going to pass more than
300 billion dollars a year in revenue,

00:44:19.278 --> 00:44:23.357
which is like 1% of the
entire gross domestic product

00:44:23.367 --> 00:44:26.253
of the United States
is spent on token spend.

00:44:26.253 --> 00:44:29.651
And this type of spend,
unlike other types of software,

00:44:29.651 --> 00:44:31.946
does not have no marginal costs.

00:44:31.946 --> 00:44:34.575
Every job you're doing
has a marginal cost.

00:44:34.575 --> 00:44:40.256
And I think in a world where before it
was fine for a CFO to go and say,

00:44:40.256 --> 00:44:43.238
"I'm just going to manage my payroll,

00:44:43.238 --> 00:44:47.072
and I have a philosophy about how I'm
going to decide how much to spend on that.

00:44:47.072 --> 00:44:48.428
I'm going to manage my vendors."

00:44:48.428 --> 00:44:51.589
I think there's going to be
a third mega category created

00:44:51.589 --> 00:44:55.375
around how we manage
knowledge work that will be done

00:44:55.375 --> 00:44:58.450
on behalf of organizations and
done through these tokens.

00:44:58.780 --> 00:45:00.528
Part of why we're so focused on this

00:45:00.528 --> 00:45:03.445
is our customers are
incredible early adopters of this.

00:45:03.445 --> 00:45:06.848
There are many reasons why Ramp
is doubling its revenue each year.

00:45:06.848 --> 00:45:09.634
This is one of them,
the growth of these categories.

00:45:09.634 --> 00:45:12.827
But these businesses are trying
to better manage the spend.

00:45:12.827 --> 00:45:17.239
One of our customers is Uber,
who was prior to going and adopting,

00:45:17.239 --> 00:45:20.985
but they had mentioned that
their CTO had said publicly

00:45:20.985 --> 00:45:22.465
that in a few months of the year,

00:45:22.465 --> 00:45:27.183
they had spent the entire budget they
had allocated for AI spend in a quarter.

00:45:27.863 --> 00:45:28.875
No one has a budget.

00:45:28.875 --> 00:45:32.825
I can tell you, five years ago, no company
budgeted for this type of spend.

00:45:33.186 --> 00:45:36.362
And by the way, a lot of
this is really high-returning,

00:45:36.362 --> 00:45:38.623
but not all spend is created equal.

00:45:38.623 --> 00:45:41.268
And so part of what we're doing
on behalf of our customers

00:45:41.268 --> 00:45:44.128
is helping them understand
where did they spend on?

00:45:44.128 --> 00:45:47.559
Was this OpEx spend?
Was this R&amp;D spend?

00:45:47.559 --> 00:45:50.026
Who spent it? What was the return on that?

00:45:50.026 --> 00:45:53.570
And I think the really interesting thing
that's happening right now is,

00:45:53.993 --> 00:45:58.125
look, most of the spend is
concentrated in frontier models, right?

00:45:58.125 --> 00:46:01.692
These are sort of a code word
for the most advanced model

00:46:01.692 --> 00:46:03.684
that is commercially accessible,

00:46:03.684 --> 00:46:06.317
and maybe you use this for
your most difficult types of asks.

00:46:06.667 --> 00:46:08.802
And the really interesting thing going on,

00:46:08.802 --> 00:46:10.788
and as this is going on
too, you have this,

00:46:11.248 --> 00:46:15.114
the labs don't like the notion
that these labs can be distilled.

00:46:15.114 --> 00:46:17.521
As you've noticed,
it's not one super model.

00:46:17.811 --> 00:46:19.951
There's a great model,
and then a few weeks later,

00:46:19.951 --> 00:46:22.039
this company comes out with
a model that's almost as good,

00:46:22.039 --> 00:46:24.615
or this company has a model
that's as good for this task or that,

00:46:24.615 --> 00:46:26.157
and it's constantly kind of moving.

00:46:26.157 --> 00:46:27.379
And what we've observed is

00:46:27.379 --> 00:46:30.751
about six months after the latest
and greatest model comes out,

00:46:30.751 --> 00:46:34.822
you'll have an open-weight
model that is just as good

00:46:35.282 --> 00:46:38.798
and maybe costs one
one-hundredth to go and use it.

00:46:38.798 --> 00:46:40.794
And so it begs the question of,

00:46:41.074 --> 00:46:44.372
you can use this advanced
alien intelligence to edit your email,

00:46:44.372 --> 00:46:50.152
but perhaps you could route these
tasks to these lower-cost models.

00:46:50.152 --> 00:46:54.330
And so part of what we
and others are focused on

00:46:54.670 --> 00:46:57.607
is delivering services like
token spend management, right?

00:46:57.607 --> 00:47:00.855
To go and observe what was
requested, what was the output, 

00:47:00.855 --> 00:47:04.311
and were there better ways
to route this type of work

00:47:04.311 --> 00:47:07.400
as it's moving outside of just
engineering to general finances.

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:09.217
And so that's a major area.

00:47:09.217 --> 00:47:10.614
There's agentic spend.

00:47:10.614 --> 00:47:13.888
One way of thinking about this is
like you and I, in some sense,

00:47:13.888 --> 00:47:16.050
are limited agents of
our companies, right?

00:47:16.050 --> 00:47:18.721
If I'm on business,
I have the limited agency

00:47:18.721 --> 00:47:21.205
to book a hotel and take
an Uber and go to dinner,

00:47:21.205 --> 00:47:24.246
but I can't just buy a 2 million dollar
Ferrari because I feel like it.

00:47:24.246 --> 00:47:27.263
I would probably need the CFO
and maybe the board sign-off.

00:47:27.263 --> 00:47:28.357
- Or if I worked with you,

00:47:28.357 --> 00:47:30.564
we're lucky enough to have
this partnership with Aman.

00:47:30.564 --> 00:47:32.926
So this film here, when we're in
New York in the beautiful Aman,

00:47:32.926 --> 00:47:34.283
I don't think you would appreciate it,

00:47:34.283 --> 00:47:36.883
if I worked at Ramp,
if I was booking the Aman Hotel.

00:47:37.122 --> 00:47:41.506
- Probably not. Unless you close some
great deals, in which case we can talk.

00:47:43.096 --> 00:47:48.627
But what I'm getting at is,
we built a structure for organizations

00:47:48.627 --> 00:47:52.794
to delegate authority to spend
on certain types of things.

00:47:52.794 --> 00:47:54.556
It is core to how the product works.

00:47:55.016 --> 00:47:58.083
And already today, there are
organizations that are saying, 

00:47:58.083 --> 00:48:03.352
"You know what? We auto-renew 10 million
dollars of software every single year."

00:48:03.352 --> 00:48:07.243
And my process today is I ask somebody
in this department or that department

00:48:07.243 --> 00:48:09.435
to go review and say,
"Do we still need it?"

00:48:09.435 --> 00:48:10.537
And they need to go ask.

00:48:10.537 --> 00:48:12.488
It's like, "Hey, we're paying
for a thousand licenses. 

00:48:12.488 --> 00:48:13.677
Who's logging in or not?"

00:48:13.677 --> 00:48:16.137
And I will submit to you
that in a few years,

00:48:16.137 --> 00:48:18.654
and frankly already we're
seeing organizations do this,

00:48:18.654 --> 00:48:20.997
are tracking this programmatically, right?

00:48:20.997 --> 00:48:24.800
They'll go and pull the data and see,
"All right, we paid for a thousand seats.

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:26.475
How many people actually logged in?"

00:48:26.635 --> 00:48:30.275
This rate that we're getting, maybe we're
getting charged 100 dollars per seat.

00:48:30.275 --> 00:48:35.861
Ramp can show them actually your
price per seat is lower or higher

00:48:35.861 --> 00:48:37.650
than what the rest of the market is doing,

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:42.726
and I think that very quickly you'll
have agents negotiating with agents

00:48:42.726 --> 00:48:44.621
to buy things on behalf of companies

00:48:44.621 --> 00:48:47.125
because, in some sense,
it's just your policies

00:48:47.125 --> 00:48:51.108
that people are applying to govern
what you're going to spend on.

00:48:51.108 --> 00:48:52.493
And I think that's going to
become software.

00:48:52.493 --> 00:48:57.114
And so part of what we're
working to do is, on one side,

00:48:57.114 --> 00:48:59.719
manage the spend where
you are actually just paying

00:48:59.719 --> 00:49:02.321
for types of compute
for all types of work.

00:49:02.321 --> 00:49:08.099
On the other side, allowing intelligence
to allocate resources for you,

00:49:08.099 --> 00:49:11.292
not just digitally log and
help you close the books,

00:49:11.292 --> 00:49:14.509
but over time, can you start
to build an operating system

00:49:14.509 --> 00:49:19.308
that allows organizations to frankly
make sure the most worthwhile cause

00:49:19.308 --> 00:49:21.793
and the highest return
on the dollar gets the dollar.

00:49:22.693 --> 00:49:25.833
- The best businesses I've studied all
figured out the same thing early.

00:49:25.833 --> 00:49:28.991
Your team is only as good as the
information they're working from.

00:49:28.991 --> 00:49:32.455
And today, that team
includes people and agents.

00:49:32.455 --> 00:49:35.564
But most generic agents
only have limited information

00:49:35.564 --> 00:49:38.385
and don't know the most
important aspects of your business,

00:49:38.385 --> 00:49:40.216
like your actual customer conversations,

00:49:40.216 --> 00:49:43.192
your sales history, what worked
last quarter, and what didn't.

00:49:43.192 --> 00:49:44.880
This is what HubSpot fixes.

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:47.253
It connects AI to your real customer data.

00:49:47.253 --> 00:49:49.858
So when you ask AI to write an email,

00:49:49.858 --> 00:49:52.482
it knows this customer asked
about pricing three weeks ago,

00:49:52.482 --> 00:49:54.513
it knows what campaign brought them in,

00:49:54.513 --> 00:49:57.531
and it knows that customer already
contacted support twice this month.

00:49:57.531 --> 00:49:59.782
And that changes everything for a startup.

00:49:59.782 --> 00:50:03.293
When you're hiring, new team
members can see how you actually sell.

00:50:03.293 --> 00:50:05.769
When you're fundraising,
your data is clean and ready.

00:50:05.769 --> 00:50:07.474
And when you're scaling,
you're building on something

00:50:07.474 --> 00:50:10.433
designed to grow with you,
not rebuilding from scratch.

00:50:10.433 --> 00:50:13.500
It's the same platform
that more than 300 startups

00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:17.309
who have grown to over a billion dollars
in valuation grew their businesses on.

00:50:17.309 --> 00:50:20.388
And now, HubSpot for Startups
offers this service

00:50:20.388 --> 00:50:24.756
at up to a 90% discount
exclusively for eligible startups.

00:50:24.756 --> 00:50:28.075
Visit hubspot.com/startups to learn more. 

00:50:28.075 --> 00:50:30.945
That is hubspot.com/startups.

00:50:31.495 --> 00:50:33.752
I just had this fascinating
conversation with Jared Kushner,

00:50:33.752 --> 00:50:39.499
and he talks about when he's trying
to negotiate these peace agreements,

00:50:39.499 --> 00:50:44.366
he doesn't start with, "Okay, let's do
the smallest thing we could do."

00:50:44.366 --> 00:50:48.111
Let's start with the most ideal situation
and work backwards from that.

00:50:48.111 --> 00:50:49.766
And he finds that he
actually has more success

00:50:49.766 --> 00:50:51.359
swinging for like the fences than if like,

00:50:51.359 --> 00:50:53.654
"Hey, I just want you to stop
doing this one little thing."

00:50:54.104 --> 00:50:55.407
- Yeah.
- My question to you is,

00:50:55.407 --> 00:50:57.690
as Ramp evolves and
these new technologies,

00:50:57.980 --> 00:51:01.440
you take advantage of all the new
latest technology developments,

00:51:01.730 --> 00:51:04.134
in your mind then, like what is...

00:51:04.134 --> 00:51:08.311
It's not there now,
but what is the ideal product?

00:51:08.651 --> 00:51:10.775
Is it literally self-driving money?

00:51:10.775 --> 00:51:16.031
It's like, you're not buying seats,
software, you're buying work.

00:51:16.031 --> 00:51:17.046
What is it to you?

00:51:17.046 --> 00:51:19.365
- I think that's really well said, right?

00:51:19.675 --> 00:51:22.048
I'll just start with who
we serve today, right?

00:51:22.048 --> 00:51:27.236
A lot of our customers are business
owners and finance organizations.

00:51:27.236 --> 00:51:30.293
And I think... I remember
meeting a lot of friends

00:51:30.293 --> 00:51:34.530
who were studying accounting
and finance back in college.

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:36.142
I remember the things
you'd hear from them,

00:51:36.142 --> 00:51:37.755
like the dreams they had is like,

00:51:37.755 --> 00:51:40.390
"I want to go be at the table
where it matters, right?

00:51:40.390 --> 00:51:44.183
I want to be allocating resources
to causes that are important.

00:51:44.183 --> 00:51:46.320
I want to help people
understand businesses better

00:51:46.320 --> 00:51:47.764
so they can make better decisions.

00:51:47.764 --> 00:51:49.448
I want to create great products.

00:51:49.958 --> 00:51:51.862
I want to do things that
are really meaningful."

00:51:51.862 --> 00:51:53.306
And you go study this kind of craft

00:51:53.306 --> 00:51:56.000
and trade of accounting,
finance, whatever it may be.

00:51:56.530 --> 00:51:58.824
And then you are rudely
awakened to the reality that's like,

00:51:58.824 --> 00:51:59.854
oh, no, no, you're not doing that.

00:51:59.854 --> 00:52:01.524
Your job is you're going
to do the expenses.

00:52:01.524 --> 00:52:04.412
You're going to go and take the bills
and you're going to go process them.

00:52:04.412 --> 00:52:06.569
You're going to go rerun this analysis

00:52:06.569 --> 00:52:09.904
that we ran last month
for a liquidity roll forward

00:52:09.904 --> 00:52:12.357
or a financial projection
again for this quarter,

00:52:12.357 --> 00:52:15.155
and you're going to spend
like 80%, 90% of your time

00:52:15.155 --> 00:52:19.643
just on rote recurring tasks because
the machinery is so hard to operate.

00:52:19.643 --> 00:52:22.181
There's so much data split
across all these tools today

00:52:22.181 --> 00:52:23.861
that is very difficult to operate.

00:52:23.861 --> 00:52:25.463
And part of what we're doing is,

00:52:25.463 --> 00:52:28.840
one, just collapsing the number
of tools that organizations need

00:52:28.850 --> 00:52:29.990
so it's easier to monitor,

00:52:29.990 --> 00:52:33.765
but two, ultimately, when you have
better tools that are generating,

00:52:33.765 --> 00:52:37.066
moving the payments,
but also have the audit trail,

00:52:37.066 --> 00:52:41.527
know your policy, is connected
to your full financial data,

00:52:41.527 --> 00:52:45.510
so it can know this dollar,
did it make you money,

00:52:45.510 --> 00:52:48.924
or was it neutral, or did you waste
money by spending on this?

00:52:50.024 --> 00:52:54.259
Then suddenly, all right,
the job starts to change

00:52:54.259 --> 00:52:57.143
from most of your time
is looking backwards

00:52:57.143 --> 00:52:59.288
and very little is actually
on the interesting stuff

00:52:59.288 --> 00:53:01.718
to finally, if you're a business owner

00:53:01.718 --> 00:53:04.726
or if you're leading the
finances of an organization,

00:53:04.726 --> 00:53:08.783
you can actually spend most of your
time on like, who is your customer?

00:53:08.783 --> 00:53:10.923
What are the great products
that we're developing for them?

00:53:10.923 --> 00:53:13.345
Where should the next
dollar actually be going,

00:53:13.345 --> 00:53:15.434
and how do we actually allocate
the resources going forward?

00:53:15.434 --> 00:53:17.995
And so I think for me,
where this turns into,

00:53:17.995 --> 00:53:21.669
and where it gets interesting is
your point is like, "Yes, it is selling."

00:53:21.669 --> 00:53:24.678
This rote low-value work is done for you.

00:53:24.998 --> 00:53:30.086
Yes, you can go and turn on
Ramp and all of these things.

00:53:30.666 --> 00:53:33.141
Maybe you're a small business owner
and you have an obsession.

00:53:33.141 --> 00:53:35.287
Maybe you know how
to make a great podcast,

00:53:35.287 --> 00:53:39.948
but you don't care at all as to like,
I do not know how to keep books

00:53:39.948 --> 00:53:44.839
or pay vendors at the lowest cost
or optimize my working capital.

00:53:44.839 --> 00:53:47.825
And you can hire Ramp to
do that for you and with you,

00:53:47.825 --> 00:53:49.117
and that's what we're working towards.

00:53:49.917 --> 00:53:54.008
I share a lot of common beliefs with
folks like Tobi who are trying to make

00:53:54.008 --> 00:53:56.484
entrepreneurship more
accessible to people.

00:53:56.484 --> 00:54:01.222
I think people should be put in a
position where technology and tools

00:54:01.222 --> 00:54:05.417
empower them to pursue their actual
missions and dreams and purposes

00:54:05.807 --> 00:54:08.717
versus getting clogged
with all the tedious work

00:54:08.717 --> 00:54:11.288
that tends to drag most people down

00:54:11.288 --> 00:54:14.112
and, I think, defines most
of corporate life today.

00:54:14.272 --> 00:54:18.539
- So I think a lot of what we've
talked about so far ties to this fact,

00:54:18.539 --> 00:54:19.801
which is surprising,

00:54:19.801 --> 00:54:23.130
and I do not know if you or Karim
have talked about this publicly,

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:28.455
but who you actually look at
as the competitors to Ramp

00:54:28.455 --> 00:54:32.932
is not the typical people
other people on the outside

00:54:32.932 --> 00:54:36.188
kind of group you together sometimes
or compare and contrast you with.

00:54:36.438 --> 00:54:40.083
Your answer to that is the labs.
- Yeah.

00:54:40.083 --> 00:54:42.395
- Can you explain why?
- Yeah.

00:54:43.415 --> 00:54:46.402
So if you get down to it,
there's this question of like,

00:54:46.402 --> 00:54:47.707
"What does Ramp sell?"

00:54:48.207 --> 00:54:53.992
And I think early on we were bunched in
with a lot of financial service providers,

00:54:53.992 --> 00:54:57.112
and I understand that comparison
kind of on the face of it.

00:54:57.112 --> 00:54:58.281
We move funds.

00:54:58.281 --> 00:55:02.082
We're quite good at it, and people are
often coming from legacy tools.

00:55:02.082 --> 00:55:06.954
And I think that these banks, at the core,

00:55:06.954 --> 00:55:10.398
a lot of what financial institutions or
folks engaged in the movement of money

00:55:10.398 --> 00:55:13.340
are selling to their end
customers is money, right?

00:55:13.340 --> 00:55:16.461
They're selling you money.
They are selling you rewards, right?

00:55:16.461 --> 00:55:18.570
A larger rebate on a purchase.

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:20.807
They are selling you more working capital.

00:55:20.807 --> 00:55:22.995
They are selling you a loan, right?

00:55:23.515 --> 00:55:26.294
They are selling you a higher
interest on your accounts,

00:55:26.294 --> 00:55:31.210
and they compete often based off
of price or brand or service

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:34.775
or some form of who can convince you
they're giving you the most money

00:55:34.775 --> 00:55:37.839
or value back and keep
a reasonable margin.

00:55:38.119 --> 00:55:41.192
Ramp was very unusual
in that, from the get-go,

00:55:41.192 --> 00:55:43.794
really what we were
trying to sell you was time.

00:55:44.594 --> 00:55:47.513
We would move your funds,
but the pain that people had

00:55:47.513 --> 00:55:50.958
was it was a waste of everyone's
time to do expense reports.

00:55:50.958 --> 00:55:54.005
It was a waste of time to
go and have to tick and tie

00:55:54.005 --> 00:55:56.591
for every payment to close your books.

00:55:56.591 --> 00:56:00.383
Really what we're trying
to go do is automate

00:56:00.383 --> 00:56:03.962
and do the work around
the money movement.

00:56:03.962 --> 00:56:07.229
It is all of the knowledge work
and the business process

00:56:07.229 --> 00:56:10.399
that we are working to automate
and help you get more value out of.

00:56:10.399 --> 00:56:14.049
In some sense, it is a form of
knowledge work and intelligence

00:56:14.049 --> 00:56:17.309
that we are serving and servicing for you.

00:56:17.309 --> 00:56:20.792
And I think that labs are, in some sense,

00:56:20.792 --> 00:56:24.457
the most comparable provider
of this type of service.

00:56:24.457 --> 00:56:27.388
I think, in some sense,
it could be labor,

00:56:27.388 --> 00:56:30.586
but it also could be knowledge
work done in different ways.

00:56:30.586 --> 00:56:33.104
And so, one, I think
it's incredibly exciting.

00:56:33.104 --> 00:56:35.941
I actually get very energized
having great competitors

00:56:36.211 --> 00:56:38.232
and having great organizations
to compete with.

00:56:38.232 --> 00:56:39.542
I think iron sharpens iron,

00:56:39.542 --> 00:56:41.980
and I think it forces people
to ask deeper questions,

00:56:41.980 --> 00:56:43.957
to get off their a** and not be lazy,

00:56:43.957 --> 00:56:46.779
and to go build something great
every single day and week.

00:56:46.779 --> 00:56:50.295
And so I think I'm having some of the
most fun I've ever had in my life.

00:56:50.295 --> 00:56:53.164
We've had five quarters in a row
of accelerating revenue growth

00:56:53.164 --> 00:56:57.519
while doubling the business and
doing it on a multibillion-dollar scale,

00:56:57.519 --> 00:56:59.832
and so that part of it has
been incredibly energizing.

00:56:59.832 --> 00:57:04.633
At the other side of it, you need to think
really carefully of these organizations,

00:57:04.633 --> 00:57:06.643
and I think humanity
is on the exponential.

00:57:06.643 --> 00:57:09.079
Just a few years ago,
we were all shocked

00:57:09.399 --> 00:57:12.664
when AI could generate a
paragraph that sort of made sense

00:57:12.664 --> 00:57:14.518
and might have had some
typos or things that were weird,

00:57:14.518 --> 00:57:15.973
but it seemed sort of human.

00:57:15.973 --> 00:57:17.878
Just a few years later, people quibble.

00:57:17.878 --> 00:57:23.384
You get a 100-page dossier,
and some footnote is off or hallucinated.

00:57:23.384 --> 00:57:26.530
And I do not think there's any
sign this stuff is slowing down.

00:57:26.530 --> 00:57:27.716
And if you really internalize

00:57:27.716 --> 00:57:30.409
what are the capabilities of
some of these organizations,

00:57:30.409 --> 00:57:32.369
I think it calls into question of,

00:57:32.369 --> 00:57:37.002
if you assume intelligence will be
functionally free in some sense,

00:57:37.002 --> 00:57:39.051
what is unique,
and how do we make sure

00:57:39.051 --> 00:57:43.149
we're adding durable and differentiated
value compared to these organizations?

00:57:43.149 --> 00:57:45.857
And so, look, it's super exciting.

00:57:45.857 --> 00:57:47.662
I think it leads us to go really deep

00:57:47.662 --> 00:57:50.564
and ensure that we are
providing a different sort of value.

00:57:50.564 --> 00:57:52.791
I think a lot of how we
do that for organizations

00:57:52.791 --> 00:57:57.154
is by being the layer where money
movement is actually occurring.

00:57:57.154 --> 00:58:01.319
I think in some orgs, maybe you can
determine two months ago we wasted funds,

00:58:01.319 --> 00:58:05.265
and with Ramp, we're building kind of
the circulation and connective tissue

00:58:05.265 --> 00:58:08.149
so you can stop dollars from
leaving before they ever go out.

00:58:08.149 --> 00:58:11.624
And so I think that it tends to feel
much more like an operating system

00:58:11.624 --> 00:58:13.353
in connecting to the movement of funds,

00:58:13.353 --> 00:58:15.974
but I think they are some of
the most exciting competitors

00:58:15.974 --> 00:58:19.119
for all types of businesses
I think we've ever seen.

00:58:19.789 --> 00:58:22.399
- I think you put forth a very
optimistic case for entrepreneurs.

00:58:22.399 --> 00:58:24.289
This was awesome.
Thanks for taking the time to do it.

00:58:24.289 --> 00:58:25.357
- Thanks a lot, David.

00:58:26.746 --> 00:58:27.867
- I hope you enjoyed this episode.

00:58:27.867 --> 00:58:31.340
Please remember to subscribe wherever
you're listening and leave a review.

00:58:31.340 --> 00:58:33.227
And make sure you listen to
my other podcast, Founders.

00:58:33.227 --> 00:58:37.096
For almost a decade, I've obsessively
read over 400 biographies

00:58:37.096 --> 00:58:38.598
of history's greatest entrepreneurs,

00:58:38.598 --> 00:58:41.242
searching for ideas that
you can use in your work.

00:58:41.242 --> 00:58:45.599
Most of the guests you hear on this show
first found me through Founders.

